<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nabopolassar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/626-bce">626</a>–605 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century-bce">7th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nabopolassar was a Chaldean leader who seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> in 626 BCE and founded the Neo-Babylonian or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaldean-empire">Chaldean Empire</a>. He took advantage of succession disputes in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a> after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashurbanipal">Ashurbanipal</a>'s death and allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyaxares">Cyaxares of Media</a>. Their coalition overwhelmed the Assyrian Empire's heartland in the 610s BCE, and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nebuchadnezzar-ii">Nebuchadnezzar II</a> succeeded him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nabopolassar.jpg" alt="Nabopolassar" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Osama Shukir Muhammed Amin FRCP(Glasg) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cylinder_of_Nabopolassar_from_Babylon,_Mesopotamia..JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The army's ultimate failure came against the Medo-Babylonian coalition of the 610s BCE, when the combined forces of Cyaxares the Mede and <b>Nabopolassar of Babylon</b> overwhelmed even Assyria's fortified heartland.</blockquote><a href="/empire/assyrian-empire">Assyrian Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Nabopolassar</b>, a leader of uncertain origin traditionally regarded as a Chaldean chieftain, seized Babylon in 626 BCE amid the turmoil that followed the death of the Assyrian king Ashurbanipal.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chaldean-empire">Chaldean Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Cyaxares (r. c. 625–585 BCE) restored Median power and reorganised the army, and in alliance with <b>Nabopolassar of Babylon</b> he destroyed Assyria, taking Ashur in 614 BCE and Nineveh in 612 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/median-empire">Median Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After the death of Ashurbanipal a struggle over the succession weakened the state, Babylon rose under <b>Nabopolassar</b>, and the Medes and Babylonians took Assur in 614, sacked Nineveh in 612, and defeated the last Assyrian attempt to retake Harran in 609.</blockquote><a href="/essays/the-collapse-problem">The Collapse Problem</a></li><li><blockquote>The Babylonian Chronicle records that <b>Nabopolassar</b> of the Chaldean Empire took Suhu in 616 BCE as he advanced against Assyria, ending its separate identity.</blockquote><a href="/state/land-of-suhu">Land of Suhu</a></li><li><blockquote>In 616 BCE Mannaean troops fought alongside Assyria against <b>Nabopolassar</b>, but after Assyria's fall the kingdom was absorbed by the Median Empire.</blockquote><a href="/state/mannaea">Mannaea</a></li></ul></section>
